{"items": [{"key": "char:dave", "name": "Dave", "source": "model", "kind": "character", "description": "A man in his late thirties, careful and methodical, who has just moved into an old house he inherited. He is a lapsed sceptic: he wants the world to be explainable, and he keeps a notebook of rational explanations that quietly fails him. He is gentle, slow to anger, and far more lonely than he admits. He passes the cemetery on his walk to the bus stop every morning.", "invariants": ["Dave only learns what he can see, hear or be told; his knowledge is limited to his own experience.", "Dave never becomes cruel; his failing is doubt and evasion, not malice."]}, {"key": "char:mira", "name": "Mira", "source": "model", "kind": "character", "description": "Dave's partner of six years. Warm, quick, and allergic to being taken in; she grew up in a family that mocked credulity, and her teasing is her way of staying in control. Her jokes about his 'houseguest' start affectionate and turn sharp as she feels him slipping away from her toward something she cannot see or compete with.", "invariants": ["Mira never sees or hears any spirit herself.", "Mira's teasing escalates gradually rather than arriving fully formed."]}, {"key": "char:eleanor", "name": "Eleanor Vane", "source": "model", "kind": "character", "description": "A woman in a grey dress who died in the house in 1908 and has never been properly mourned. She is courteous, dry, watchful, and deeply tired of being a rumour. She speaks softly, chooses her words, and asks for respect rather than belief: she wants to be addressed, not proved. She is drawn to Dave because he is the first person in a century to say her name aloud without laughing.", "invariants": ["Eleanor never appears to Mira.", "Eleanor cannot leave the house and the strip of cemetery visible from its upper windows."]}, {"key": "place:hollis_house", "name": "The Hollis House", "source": "model", "kind": "place", "description": "A narrow three-storey house at the end of a terraced street, inherited from a great-aunt. Its sash windows are streaked with a century of rain-marks, so the light inside comes through in grey stripes. Dim hallways run front to back on each floor, papered in faded green, with a smell of cold plaster. Furniture in it shifts on its own: chairs turned a quarter-turn, a wardrobe crowding the door, drawers opening in sequence.", "invariants": ["The windows stay streaked; cleaning them never fully works.", "The hallways remain dim in daylight.", "Furniture never moves while it is being directly watched."]}, {"key": "place:cemetery", "name": "The Cemetery Path", "source": "model", "kind": "place", "description": "A hillside burial ground between the house and the bus stop. Dave passes it every morning and evening. It is misty most days, with leaning stones, a broken iron railing and yew trees that hold the damp. The newest graves belong to 1908 and earlier; the far gate has been welded shut.", "invariants": ["Dave crosses or skirts the cemetery on his daily commute.", "Mist is present on most visits."]}, {"key": "rule:manifestation", "name": "How the house shows itself", "source": "model", "kind": "rule", "description": "The haunting follows fixed rules: furniture shifts only when unobserved; shadow figures are seen at the edge of vision and are gone when looked at directly; the spirits are quietest when spoken to politely and loudest when mocked; a name, once said aloud with respect, cannot be unsaid.", "invariants", ["Mockery makes the manifestations worse, respect makes them quieter.", "Shadow figures never resolve into clear faces.", "No spirit can be proved to a second witness."]}, {"key": "line:teasing_wedge", "name": "The wedge of a joke", "source": "model", "kind": "conflictLine", "description": "Mira's teasing and Dave's refusal to defend himself widen into a shared room where neither says what they mean. Every laugh at his expense makes the house louder and his account of it less tellable, until the joke is the only language they have left.", "invariants": ["The wedge widens chapter by chapter and never resets to intimacy."]}, {"key": "line:disbelief_and_desire", "name": "Torn between disbelief and desire", "source": "model", "kind": "conflictLine", "description": "Dave's longing for Eleanor collides with his conviction that a wanting ghost cannot be real, and with his loyalty to Mira. He wants the encounter to have been madness and wants it to have been true, and both wants cost him something.", "invariants": ["Dave never fully resolves the question of whether Eleanor is real.", "His desire is never simply triumphant; it is always shadowed by guilt."]}, {"key": "fw:voice", "name": "Narration", "source": "model", "kind": "frameworkChoice", "description": "Third person limited, past tense, anchored in Dave, with restrained literary ghost-story prose: concrete sensory detail, short chapters, and no explanation of the supernatural beyond what Dave can observe. Domestic dialogue carries the marital conflict; the haunting is conveyed through sound, temperature and things found moved.", "invariants": ["No omniscient confirmation of what the spirits are.", "The prose stays within Dave's senses and inferences."]}, {"key": "rel:dave->mira", "name": "Dave and Mira", "source": "model", "kind": "relationBaseline", "description": "Six years together, affectionate but tired, held together by habit and jokes. They have never lived anywhere they chose together. Mira's teasing is love and control at once; Dave's silence is patience and cowardice at once."}, {"key": "rel:dave->eleanor", "name": "Dave and Eleanor", "source": "model", "kind": "relationBaseline", "description": "Begins as host and presence, becomes something like courtship conducted through courtesy and small rituals, then a single night of tenderness. Eleanor asks for respect; Dave gives it before he believes, which is what draws her."}]}
